<Page title="a head made, not chosen" description="Personal binaural from the average of measured heads and two sliders.">
  <p class="label"><a href={`${base}/research`}>research</a></p>
  <h1>a head made, not chosen</h1>
  <p class="sub label">personal binaural from the average of measured heads, an ear slider and a head slider</p>

  <div class="prose paper">
    <section id="abstract">
      <h2>abstract</h2>
      <p>
        Most people hear binaural audio through one dummy head, the Neumann KU100. bambi’s decoder offers a head of
        your own for almost no effort: two sliders, one for the size of the head and one for the ears, over a head
        made from the average of 268 measured heads. The timbre stays the KU100’s; what changes is where sounds seem
        to come from. The aim is a quick gain over the dummy, not the reconstruction of anyone’s measured head.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="aim">
      <h2>the aim</h2>
      <p>
        A measured head — in a loudspeaker array, or from a scan — is the most accurate, and out of reach for most
        listeners. The dummy head is the opposite: one head for everyone, and for many people sounds that sit inside
        the head or flip from front to back. The decoder aims between the two: a head that beats the dummy after a
        few minutes of listening, found by ear, with nothing to measure and no file to load.
      </p>
      <p>
        Heads are taken as <strong>symmetric</strong>: one ear over the whole sphere is a head, and the other ear its
        mirror. This halves what the model has to describe and makes every measured head two: its left ear mirrored,
        and its right. A head’s two ears differ by two thirds of what two different heads differ by, so the two are
        distinct heads rather than copies.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="data">
      <h2>the data</h2>
      <p>
        The model is learned from two public databases, both under CC BY 4.0: <strong>HUTUBS</strong>, 96 people
        measured over the whole sphere, and <strong>3D3A</strong>, 38 people. Mirrored, they give 192 and 76
        symmetric heads. The <strong>KU100</strong> comes from SADIE II, measured on 8802 directions. A third, larger
        database, SONICOM, is not learned from: its heads serve only to check the model on people it has never seen.
      </p>
      <p>Databases differ in how they measure more than in whom they measure, so they are made comparable first:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <strong>One grid.</strong> Every head is fitted with spherical harmonics up to order 20, with a penalty on
          roughness, so that a database without measurements below the listener fills that gap from the population’s
          average rather than from any one head.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One level.</strong> Every head is equalised onto the KU100’s transfer function, ear by ear. The
          databases’ average levels, up to 17.6 dB apart before, end 2.1 to 2.5 dB apart: less than heads differ
          within one database.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>One average.</strong> Each database’s mean is moved onto the mean of all of them, each database
          weighted equally, with the spread between its heads kept. After that, a linear classifier names a head’s
          database only at chance. Two random halves of one database differ by under 1.1 dB, a third of the gap
          between databases, so what is removed is measurement, not people.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section id="parts">
      <h2>a head in parts</h2>
      <p>A symmetric head is stored as the parts of what the two ears hear:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>the timbre</strong>: the summed level of the two ears, averaged over every direction. It is the
          head’s colour, not a cue for where a sound is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>the smooth pattern</strong>: how the summed level changes over the sphere, as spherical harmonics up
          to order 4 — the head and the torso.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>the notches</strong>: three dips the outer ear cuts into the high frequencies, each a centre, a
          depth, a width and a presence over the sphere. They carry most of what tells above from below.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>the level difference</strong> between the ears (order 10) and <strong>the time difference</strong>
          (order 8), which carry left from right.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Every part is a pattern over the sphere, so a direction is evaluated rather than looked up, and no measured
        grid is shipped. Along frequency, the parts are sampled every tenth of an ERB.
      </p>
      <figure class="chart" data-figure="parts">
        <div class="controls"><label><span class="label">azimuth</span><input type="range" data-k="az" min="-180" max="180" step="5" value="30" data-unit="°" /><output></output></label><label><span class="label">elevation</span><input type="range" data-k="el" min="-45" max="90" step="5" value="0" data-unit="°" /><output></output></label></div>
        <div class="plot"><canvas aria-label="figure 1: The parts of nobody’s head at one direction: the summed level of the two ears, its smooth pattern with the timbre, the notches cut into it, and the level difference between the ears. Move the direction."></canvas></div>
        <figcaption><span class="label">figure 1</span> The parts of nobody’s head at one direction: the summed level of the two ears, its smooth pattern with the timbre, the notches cut into it, and the level difference between the ears. Move the direction.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="nobodys-head">
      <h2>nobody’s head</h2>
      <p>
        Every part but the notches is the population’s mean: <strong>nobody’s head</strong>, the average of the 268
        heads, each database’s mean taken first and then the mean of those. It is no one’s, and closer to anyone’s
        than the dummy is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its <strong>timbre is the KU100’s</strong>. The dummy’s is flat within 3.4 dB from 0.3 to 16 kHz; the
        population’s spans 12.9 dB, up at 4 kHz and down at 16 kHz — the ear’s own resonance, which every measured
        head carries and the dummy’s equalisation takes out. In the perceptual study this model starts from [1], an
        individualised head won on externalisation and lost on timbre; keeping the dummy’s timbre keeps the colour a mix is judged on, and personalises only the
        cues for direction.
      </p>
      <figure class="chart" data-figure="timbre">
        <div class="plot"><canvas aria-label="figure 2: The timbre — the summed level averaged over every direction — of the KU100, which the model keeps, and of the measured heads, which it does not."></canvas></div>
        <figcaption><span class="label">figure 2</span> The timbre — the summed level averaged over every direction — of the KU100, which the model keeps, and of the measured heads, which it does not.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="ear-slider">
      <h2>the ear slider</h2>
      <p>
        The notches are a <strong>template</strong> of three notch surfaces over the sphere — centre, depth, width
        and presence, each as spherical harmonics of order 6 — learned from half of the heads and scored on the
        other half. A larger ear cuts its notches lower, a smaller one higher, so the slider shifts all three
        centres together along frequency, by −2 to +2 ERB. A measured head’s own shift spreads by 0.58 ERB.
      </p>
      <p>
        A notch fades in with its presence, so nothing switches as a source moves; in front, the template plays its
        notches at about 7.0, 9.2 and 13.7 kHz. They play at 1.5 times the depth the heads measure: deep enough to
        be heard as height, without the darkening a deeper template brings. The template fits SONICOM’s heads, which
        it never saw, as well as its own held-out ones.
      </p>
      <figure class="chart" data-figure="ears">
        <div class="controls"><label><span class="label">ears</span><input type="range" data-k="shift" min="-2" max="2" step="0.1" value="0.8" data-unit=" ERB" /><output></output></label><label><span class="label">elevation</span><input type="range" data-k="el" min="-45" max="90" step="5" value="0" data-unit="°" /><output></output></label></div>
        <div class="plot"><canvas aria-label="figure 3: The ear slider at work: the summed level in front, at an elevation, as learned and with the notches shifted. Move the ears, and the height."></canvas></div>
        <figcaption><span class="label">figure 3</span> The ear slider at work: the summed level in front, at an elevation, as learned and with the notches shifted. Move the ears, and the height.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="head-slider">
      <h2>the head slider</h2>
      <p>
        A larger head puts the ears further apart. The head slider, from 0.75 to 1.3, <strong>scales the time
        difference</strong> between the ears, and the level difference below 1.5 kHz goes with it, as it does across
        the measured heads; above, it is left alone. A measured head’s own scale spreads by about 9 %.
      </p>
      <p>
        Each slider moves its own cues. At 60° to the left, the ears ±10 % move the level difference by 1.6 dB at
        most, while the head ±10 % moves the time difference by ±70 µs and the summed level by 0.05 dB at most.
      </p>
      <figure class="chart" data-figure="head">
        <div class="controls"><label><span class="label">head</span><input type="range" data-k="size" min="0.75" max="1.3" step="0.01" value="1.15" data-unit=" ×" /><output></output></label></div>
        <div class="plot"><canvas aria-label="figure 4: The head slider at work: the time difference between the ears around the horizon, for the population’s head and a head of the slider’s size."></canvas></div>
        <figcaption><span class="label">figure 4</span> The head slider at work: the time difference between the ears around the horizon, for the population’s head and a head of the slider’s size.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="rendering">
      <h2>rendering a direction</h2>
      <p>
        To play a direction, the decoder evaluates every part there: the summed level from the timbre, the smooth
        pattern and the shifted notches; the two ears from it and the level difference; both made minimum-phase;
        and the pair delayed by the time difference, less what the minimum-phase pair already carries. Rendering the
        KU100 through the same chain, at its own size, comes within 0.60 dB (median) of its measured magnitudes and
        3 µs of its measured time difference, over 300 directions.
      </p>
      <p>
        A slider move rebuilds the filters off the audio thread: 240 directions at 513 frequencies take 1.28 ms on
        one core. The model ships as one file of about 0.36 MB.
      </p>
      <figure class="chart" data-figure="ku100">
        <div class="controls"><label><span class="label">azimuth</span><input type="range" data-k="az" min="-180" max="180" step="5" value="135" data-unit="°" /><output></output></label><label><span class="label">elevation</span><input type="range" data-k="el" min="-45" max="90" step="5" value="30" data-unit="°" /><output></output></label></div>
        <div class="plot"><canvas aria-label="figure 5: Nobody’s head beside the KU100, both through the decoder’s chain, at one direction. Move the direction."></canvas></div>
        <figcaption><span class="label">figure 5</span> Nobody’s head beside the KU100, both through the decoder’s chain, at one direction. Move the direction.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section id="limits">
      <h2>limits</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          A head’s own smooth pattern, the detail of its level difference, and the paths its notches take over
          direction are not in the model: no slider reaches them.
        </li>
        <li>
          The time difference’s shape — how it rises from the front to the side, any asymmetry between front and back —
          is not personalised; the head slider only scales it.
        </li>
        <li>
          The sliders are found by ear. A page for doing that against a real loudspeaker is still to be built, and
          the model is still to be heard by a round of listeners.
        </li>
        <li>
          Earbuds in transparency mode change the very cues the sliders move, so personalising a head is done on open
          headphones.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="references">
      <h2>references</h2>
      <ol class="references">
